  "image_name": "0e07ef2c0c8beb9c34c9d4c68aa0fffa.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure uses a “mobile medical unit” as a visual metaphor for the full workflow of field-based pathogen genomics, showing how many linked factors affect whether sequencing leads to useful clinical or public health decisions. A patient and healthcare worker at one side represent the starting conditions: the host, the pathogen, and the sample itself. Visible labels emphasize variables such as genome size, phase of infection, pathogen levels, sample type, and preservation, suggesting that biological context strongly shapes what can be detected.\n\nInside the tent, the process moves through laboratory handling and sequencing. Bench work highlights sample processing issues, including extraction efficiency, enrichment, library preparation, and contamination. A second station with a computer and sequencer points to technical trade-offs in sequencing, such as cost, portability, instrument performance, and coverage depth.\n\nThe workflow then extends into bioinformatics, where computing power, internet access, reference databases, related species, analysis pipeline performance, and confidence thresholds influence interpretation. Finally, a clinician holding a clipboard symbolizes the end goal: an interpretable report that supports clear clinical or epidemiological action. Overall, the image stresses that successful mobile sequencing depends on the entire chain, not just the sequencing device.",
